  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow level easterly flow continues to support low stratus and areas \nof dense fog development over central and north-central MT, while a \nmoist southwesterly flow aloft overruns the cooler air mass and \nsends waves of light showers, sprinkles, and even some isolated \ninstances of freezing drizzle. This general pattern will more or \nless persist through Sunday before a closed mid -level low over the \nPacific NW heads towards the Northern Rockies and brings more \nwidespread rain and snow Sunday through early Monday. The fog \nimpacts will be most widespread over central and north-central areas \nduring the overnight and early morning hours. \n\nThe closed low will begin migrating towards the Northern Rockies \nlate Saturday night into Sunday while being weakened and elongated \nby synoptic shearing. With the colder air aloft not scheduled to \narrive until Sunday night, snow levels will be over 7000 ft for most \nlocations, except along the Continental Divide. This will bring \nmostly liquid precipitation for lower elevations with the exception \nof some isolated instances of light freezing drizzle over the plains \nwest of I15. Some freezing precipitation may also occur for the \ncolder southwest valleys along the MT/ID/WY borders.\n\nH700 temperatures finally fall to around -5C by Sunday night into \nearly Monday and this will bring more efficient accumulating snow, \nheaviest over the Madison/Gallatin ranges above 6000 ft. There will \nbe a brief period with snow levels falling to the valley/plains \nfloors Monday morning, although it does not appear that this will be \nlong enough to support widespread accumulating snow impacts. \nNorthwesterly winds may get gusty Monday morning, though gusts look \nto stay below 40 mph. Ensembles continue to favor ridging aloft to \nbring drier conditions and near average temperatures for the \nremaining first half of next week, with unsettled conditions looking \nto return later in the week. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n...Dense fog through Sunday...\n\nHi-Res Ensemble Forecast (HREF) guidance supports the development of \nfog between 9pm this evening and 3am Sunday over the plains of \nCentral and North Central Montana, with this area of fog persisting \nthrough 9am to 3pm Sunday before an area of precipitation lifting \nnortheast from Southwest Montana brings an end to the reduced \nvisibilities. The aforementioned HREF guidance largely support \nprobabilities of between 40-60% with respect to visibilities \nfalling below 1/4 mile during the overnight hours, and for this \nreason a Dense Fog Advisory has been issued yet again through 12 \npm Sunday. Confidence is highest in visibilities falling to 1/4 \nmile or less along and west of the I-15 corridor, MT Hwy 200/US \nHwy 87 corridor, and in Milk River Valley where persistent low \nlevel east to north flow will support upslope fog. \n- Moldan\n\n...Light mountain snow and lower elevation rain, snow, and some \nmixed precipitation Sunday through Monday morning...\n\nThe main trough moves through Sunday through early Monday and brings \na wave of widespread precipitation to much of the forecast area. \nAside from some light snow along the Continental Divide and perhaps \nsome spotty freezing drizzle over the plains west of I15 and over \nthe higher southwest valleys, most of the precipitation will be \nfalling in the form of rain until Sunday night. The overall pattern \nand NBM probabilities favor the Madison and Gallatin ranges for the \nbest chance (60 to 80%) for snowfall amounts in excess of 3 inches. \nLighter snows, generally up to a couple inches, look to fall along \nthe Continental Divide and the central island ranges. The colder air \nis still not looking to be optimally aligned with the moisture for \nlower elevation snows, but will monitor the Gallatin valley where \nthere is around a 30 to 40% chance for a couple inches of snow on \nthe south/eastern fringes of the valley Monday morning. - RCG",
